      "text": "Since the triangle is a right triangle, we can consider it as a scaled version of the 3-4-5 Pythagorean triple.\nWe can use the triple ratio to find the length of the hypotenuse, which is\n\\[h = \\log_4 27 \\cdot \\log_2 9.\\]\nSince multiplication is commutative, we can also write $\\log_2 9$ as $\\log_{16} 81.$\nNow, we can rewrite $\\log_4 27$ as $\\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}$ and $\\log_{16} 81$ as $\\frac{\\log_2 81}{\\log_2 16} = \\frac{4 \\log_2 3}{4} = \\log_2 3.$\nTherefore,\n\\[h = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\cdot \\log_2 3 = \\frac{3 \\log_2 9}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 = 3.\\]\nFinally, we can compute $4^h$ as\n\\[4^h = 4^3 = \\boxed{64}.\\]\n\nWe can also solve this problem by first finding the legs of the right triangle. Since the legs are in proportional form to the sides of the 3-4-5 triangle, we can write the equation\n\\[\\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 9} = \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{\\text{leg 1}}{\\text{leg 2}}.\\]\nSolving for the legs, we find\n\\[\\text{leg 1} = \\log_2 27 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}, \\qquad \\text{leg 2} = \\log_2 9 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}.\\]\nUsing the identity $\\log_a b + \\log_a c = \\log_a (a b c),$ we can combine the two expressions into a single logarithm:\n\\[\\text{leg 1} + \\text{leg 2} = (\\log_2 27 + \\log_2 9) = \\log_2 (27 \\cdot 9) = \\log_2 243.\\]\nFinally, we can use the identity $\\log_a (ab) = \\log_a a + \\log_a b$ to find the value of $\\log_2 4^h:$\n\\[\\log_2 4^h = \\log_2 (4^3) = \\log_2 4 + \\log_2 4^3 = 2 + 3 = 5.\\]\nTherefore, we know that $\\log_2 4^h = 5,$ so $4^h = \\boxed{64}.$\nThe answer is: 64",
